The victory of right-wing populists in Austria with a total of 29%
Deplorable declining the two parties of the Grand Coalition so-of-peasant grand coalition and a significant strengthening of right-wing populist parties, according to the first preliminary results, the early parliamentary elections in Austria on Sunday.
The Austrian People’s Party (ÖVP), where the position of the current president Wilhelm Molterer was seriously shaken, suffered a particularly strong loss.
On the other hand, the president of the Association for the Future of Austria (BZÖ), the provincial magistrate of Karintia, Jörg Haider, is preparing for a victorious return to national politics.
Preliminary results show that the country’s strongest party will remain with 29.8 per cent of the country’s Democratic Party (SPÖ), but has lost 5.5 percent of the vote compared to the most recent elections in 2006.
The ÖVP was even worse, losing nearly nine percent of the vote with 25.6%.
These are the worst achievements achieved so far in the post-war history of both major parties, and in particular in the People’s Party, which caused early elections, the result is literally catastrophic.
At the beginning of July, when leaving the ÖVP coalition, the People’s Party had a significant advantage in front of the SPÖ according to opinion polls.
Voters were visibly penalised for letting the government fall.
Spectacular speculate about the possible resignation of party chairman Wilhelm Molterer and current Vice-Chancello-Chancellor in the first reactions to the results.
Observers believe this could take place at an extraordinary meeting of the party presidency on Monday.
This development is likely to facilitate an increasingly probable way out of the electoral result, i.e. the refurbishment of the SPÖ and ÖVP Grand Coalition.
In view of the strengthening of the two right-wing populist parties – the Austrian Freedom Party (FPÖ) had previously won 18% of the vote and the BZÖ received 11 percent of the votes, but the Social-Democrats expressed their fears about the recurrence of 1999, when the People’s Party reached an agreement with the populists on a common government (the FPÖ was still unequivocally in 2005).
Haider, who has already stated that he is ready to cooperate with any of the parties and assuming he can return to the national politics of Vienna, obviously counts on this solution.
The Green Party also came to the parliament, which, however, slightly lost its popularity and fell from third to fifth among the Austrian political parties.
None of the other parties exceeded the four-per-period and did not make it to parliament.
