In addition to the experts of the German Federal Army, GSG 9 (GSG 9), the German Federal Police Airport, and the Federal Criminal Office, as well as logistical experts of the German Technical Service, were also at the site of the rescue.
According to ministerial spokesmen on Monday evening, however, German forces were not needed, as the hostage-takers let their captrins run and then fled when they recognized the signs of a violent hostage rescue.
The Egyptian government accepted the assistance offered by Germany in order to involve it as an on-the-spot support force.
Luckily, it didn’t happen, a spokesman for the Federal Ministry of the Interior said in a statement.
The attackers acquitted a group of tourists from the five Germans, five Italians, a Romanian woman and eight of their Egyptian companions in southern Egypt on 19 September.
After the kidnapping in the South-Egypt desert area, the hostages were deported back and forth in the border areas of Egypt, Libya, Sudan and Chad.
The identities of the hostage-takers have remained unclear so far, as well as the details of the release of the hostages or possible ransom payments.
In the meantime, those who had been rescued arrived safely in Cairo.
Steinmeier said the rescued hostages were under the protection of the German embassy.
