The organizers are registering more and more bookings.
It is a good winter for German tour operators.
According to a study, the turnover of road seizures for the coming months increased significantly compared to the previous year – by more than 30 percent for the winter season.
In spite of the weak cyclical prospects, according to the study, the desire to travel of the Germans did not deteriorate, according to the study.
Travel agencies recorded a 31 per cent increase in traffic compared to the previous year’s period for bookings for the winter season, the German Travel Association (DRV) said on Monday in Berlin.
According to GfK market research institute’s investigation, the number of bookings in the autumn months is already higher.
The turnover of the travel agencies in October was around 11 percent higher than in October 2007, the DRV announced.
These results are based on GfK’s data, which evaluates the monthly booking data of 1,200 German travel agencies and then extravaganizes the entire market.
The institute took into account the trips booked until the end of August 2008.
Due to the weak dollar exchange rate, trips to the U.S. in particular are incompetitive, the DRV said.
The number of bookings has risen by about one-third.
The particularly popular destinations of the Germans are in Spain, Turkey and Greece, as well as domestic landscapes.
“Ordinary trips are mostly in demand,” DRV President Klaus Laepple said.
Due to the rising energy and food costs, in the case of organised trips, the costs "can be more calculated for passengers than for individually organised trips."
Among other things, passengers will be able to save money with pre-booking discounts or all-inclusive offers, Laepple added.
The booking rate of the summer months was also very good, so it will be possible to record an increase in traffic again compared to the previous year – although according to another study, fewer German tourists undertook to travel compared to the previous year.
