Turkey's Expensive diplomatic horizon
Long before Turkey intends to join the European Union, the European powers ignited the desire to penetrate deep into the Turkish hinterland.
On the eastern side of Bosphorus, perhaps only 20 steps from the European border, stands one of the greatest relics of the failed imperial aspiration.
The Hydarpasha train station was designed by Otto Ritter and Helmut Conu in neo-renaissance.
At least that's what the guidebooks say.
The building surrounds the ends of the platforms with a torment at the front corner of each of them, and looks like a German rural town hall.
The stone's dirty.
The whole building's a little bulging.
However, contemplating the vaulted ticket office, it is obvious what large-scale undertaking this was the westernmost gateway.
The station was opened by the German consortium Anatolia-Baghdad Corporation in August 1908.
“Delay the future of confiscations” wanted to achieve its ambitions aimed at capturing the Middle-Earth-Earhumidian railway from Damascus through Baghdad to Saudi Arabia.
German confiscation aspirations collapsed during World War I.
His railroads have been invaded by Lawrence of Arabia and its Arab partisans.
Relative Isolation from the surrounding regions, caused by the frozen borders of the Cold War, has vanished.
The British colonial army withdrew from Egypt and expelled the Turks and their German allegiations from Palestine and Syria.
Hyderpasha station didn't get any better.
In 1917 an ammunition train exploded, destroying the beautifully crafted velocity of the building.
According to Turkish sources, a sabotage operation took place.
Since then, it has slowly deteriorated, and history has gone well.
At the same time today, as the gateway to the Anatoly and the entire eastern region, at least in a symbolic sense, Turkey serves as an expanding diplomatic horizon.
“The Justice and Development Party in government—the AKP—developed a completely new foreign policy for the country.
Relative Isolation from the surrounding regions, caused by the frozen borders of the Cold War, has vanished.
The new policy is characterised by commitment.
It is noteworthworthy that the Turkish government maintains good relations with Israel and Syria, the Palestinian groups of Hamas and Fatah, Iraq, Iran and, of course, the European Union and the United States.
I wanted to assess the parameters of the new foreign policy, so I got on the night train to the Turkish capital, Ankara, to visit one of the leading foreign policy experts of the AKP, the Turkish MP Suat Kiniklioglu.
The next morning, after about ten and a half hours of bubbling, which at that time also gave the rhythm of the famous documentary "The Night Mail" about the old world - I arrived in the Turkish capital. The new city is intended to be a symbol of the new secular state, which turns its backs on the Ottoman past.
However, the well-know-of-the-art AKP represents a paradigm-shift change in the Turkish Consensus.
This sparked some suspicion in the West, especially in Washington.
I took a taxi to the headquarters of the AKP, an ultra-developing, made of stone and steel, a specially crafted building in a developing sub-urban district between half-finished shopping malls and coffeehouses. In the latter, middle-leaders in the suit – both men and women – come to do business early in the morning.
Suat Kiniklioglu would have been appropriated for a centre-left-responsible politician in any other European country.
He was wearing a suit and a tie, and I'm pretty sure he's got another Blackberry.
“The party’s foreign policy goal is to help Turkey reintegrate into its immediate environment.
“The Cold War has overthrode order,” he said.
Turkey has been isolated from its hinterland.
“Today it had to turn east and west at the same time.
He couldn't choose between the two.
He had to open in both directions.
“But maybe sooner or later he’ll be forced to make a choice?” he asked.
“Turkey never wanted to be at a crossroads.”
He then emphasized what almost everyone with whom I spoke here, almost mechanically repeated: Turkey’s trejoals not only with the Middle-Eastern, but the Caucasus (Caucasian) and the countries surrounding the Black Sea also benefit Europe and the United States, as well as Turkey itself.
It’s as if they’re trying to convey the message that, figuratively, together with Turkey, the whole of Europe can get to Asia and the Middle East along those east-death railway lines.
When I was again at Hydarpasha's station, after another, mostly waking-fight night, I was thinking of Herr Ritter and Herr Conura, the two German gentlemans who witnessed the recovery of this ordefinite culmination 100 years ago.
It would have been astonished to find out that Europe’s ambitions in the Middle-Eastern are now largely in the hands of Turkey.
And as I waited in the harrbour to take you back through the Bosphorus, it occurred to me that at that time Turkey was called the "sick man in Europe."
At the same time, there was no question about which continent should claim this exciting country.
Vernon Handley and William Woodruff
Mark Brown writes: “Since in 1962 he has led the Guildford Philharmonic Orchestra and Kórus as the musical director of Guildford.”
Based on his musical plans for the part of the city, the following year he founded his wonderful youth choir, Proteus.
Only those under the age of 21 might have belonged to the choir, but when Proteus sang their finals in 1979 – many of us were already at the end of our 20s—we just didn’t want to get rid of the choir.
"Proteus" (the mythological shape-shifting name) was in accordance with the choir's ability to cope with any sheet of sheet music they put in front of it - whether it's madrigal, or a Messiaen piece; unaccompanied or accompanied by an orchestra.
The musical versatility of such a young choir was often astonishing and always exciting, and we owe it to the man whose enthusiasm for music has remained unchanged over the years.
How often Tod told us, "Let's let the music sound on its own."
He always said, “The composer knows what he wants.
It's all in the sheet music.
“Speak it.”
His conductor’s technique has always been completely understandable and accurate, but it is never dictatorial or exaggerated.
No matter what the level of music, he's always lifted on it.
