William Woodruff
Professor John Beckett writes:
According to the obituary of William Woodruff (September 25-September), “he continued his studies in economic history at Oxford in 1946, and in 1950 he became a scholarship holder at the Houblon-Norman research institute... while he was constantly preparing for a bachelor’s degree, a master’s diploma and a Ph.D.
This comes from Woodruff's "Beyond Nab End," but it's erroneous.
Between 1946 and 1953, he taught economic history at the University of Nottingham, and under the leadership of Professor J.D. Chambers, he completed his PhD dissertation on the British rubber industry, which was then published in 1958 under the title "The British Compulsive Industry".
In 1950-51 he actually resided in the U.S. with a Houblon-Norman Scholarship, and in the summer of 1952 at Illinois, New York and Harvard universities.
In 1953 he finally left Nottingham and moved to Illinois.
We don’t know why Woodruff has beautified this period of his career.
