For the second time, sales record at Honda
In November, Honda will begin the second-to-generation Jazz.
It’s true that the passenger compartment of its predecessor was already large enough, but the interior design of the interior of the new model is even more spacious.
All this is coupled with sophisticated mechanics, which makes it more convenient to drive and increases fuel savings.
Evolution versus revolution.
That’s what the second-generation Honda Jazz looks like.
Although the view of the new Jazz is very similar to that of its predecessor, a number of internal changes have been made.
These include innovative design, the new body, the larger dimensions and the new mechanics.
However, before we move to a detailed description of the new model, we have to say that the new Jazz appeared six years after the first generation has been launched on the market.
First of all, it can be stated that Honda succeeded innovating this model in a shorter period of time than by other manufacturers.
In Europe, Jazz is less well-known, but it breaks sales records in other markets.
For example, Japan is the most sought-after car brand, where Jazz was the best-selling car brand in 2002 and this year in 2008.
In Japan, for example, nearly a million pieces of Jazz have been shipwrecked since it was launched.
The second market was the Old Continent, with nearly half a million copies sold in six years.
However, with the new Jazz, Honda wants to further increase the traffic in the region.
In other words, while in 2007 the Japanese company sold a total of 83,000 Jazz, by 2009 it would aim to sell 100,000 cars, resulting in a 17% increase in the European market.
In the Spanish market since 2002, Honda has sold 8,000 Jazz, accounting for 6.7% of Honda’s total turnover in Spain.
In the case of the new generation, the managers of the Spanish subsidiary calculate with 2,000 copies sold over the course of a year.
Moreover, the arrival of the new Jazz will significantly alleviate Honda’s traffic in Spain.
Representatives of the company are predicting 22,000 copies sold by the end of this year, which is 4,000 fewer than in 2007, i.e. a 16% decrease.
In spite of the selling conditions, Honda increased its market share in Spain, rising to 1.8% from 1.6% last year.
They would also like to increase this rate to 2% by 2010.
Honda hopes to do this with the introduction of a new model.
This would be the Insight, a five-door-and-a-compact – 4,370 millimetre-long – hybrid, of which the Japanese company would sell 200,000 units a year worldwide.
According to the Japanese manufacturer, the United States accounted for 50% of Insight’s market distribution, while the remainder would be distributed between the Old Continent and Japan.
The commercial introduction of Insight is due after an official demonstration scheduled for January in the first months of the year.
Compared to the first generation, the new Jazz became more spacious in every way.
Its length reaches 3,900 millimetres (five inches longer), has a width of 1,690 mm (two centimetres wider) and the wheelbase has increased five centimetres, i.e. 2,500 mm.
The altitude, on the other hand, remains 15,220 mm.
In addition to the dimensions, the passenger compartment has also become more spacious.
On the one hand, the larger interior is guaranteed by the new bodywork and, on the other hand, the fuel tank placed in the middle – this solution can already be known from the previous Jazz.
In addition, the total cargo area of the so-called double-floor trunk has also increased, currently 399 litres, i.e. 19 litres above the capacity of the first generation.
At the same time, these sizes apply to the 1.4-engine-enabled-engine model, as the 1.2-litre Honda Jazz has 379-litre trunks.
You can also discover some external modifications, such as rear led lights or a panoramic glass roof, as well as easier accessibility of the rear seats.
In addition, the harness angle of the doors belonging to the rear seats was raised to 80 degrees, as opposed to the 67-degree opening angle of the first Jazz.
The second-to-generation Jazz engines are also new.
At the beginning of its propulsion, this small model can be equipped with two 1.2-litre and 1.4-litre engines.
Thanks to the i-VTEC technology, the fuel consumption of both engines is more favorable than its previous model and has less CO2 emissions and excellent mechanical alignment, while the engine delivers outstanding performance in all respects.
The 1.2-litre engine delivers 90 horsepower at 6,000 rpm with a maximum torque of 114 Nm 4900 rpm.
This engine, which had 78 horsepower in the previous Jazz, consumes 5.3 litres of CO2 and 125 grams of CO2 emissions.
The other engine, which produced 83 horsepower in the previous generation, now reaches 100 horsepower.
The 100-horsepower 1.4 Jazz consumes an average of 5.4 litres, with an emissions of 128 grams of CO2.
Custraising "bee car"
For the development of accident-preventive technologies, this time Nissan drew inspiration from its new incomprehensible from nature, namely the world of bees.
The Japanese company has designed a robot that migrates the beehive of bees.
The Bio-mimetic Car Robot Drive or BR23C device may be the first step in developing collision prevention technologies.
Engineers at Nissan Motor’s Co. Developed Technical Center in collaboration with the renowned Tokyo University’s Advanced Science and Technology Research Centre have built this small robot in Japan.
Kazuhhiro Doi, Director of the Technology Public Relations and Renewal of Technology and R&D, defines the BR23C as an additional extension of Nissan’s four-tier safety cover.
The robot completes the collision avoidability of the company’s products.
Every bee forms an oval-shaped personal space, which is very similar to Nissan’s safety barrier.
What is more important, however, is more important for the uterus’ complex eyes, with which you can see at an angle of more than 300-destroke in all directions, and as a result you can fly undisturbed in the uterus’ own personal space.
To replicate the function of the complex eye, the engineers designed a so-called laser telemeter (Laser Range Finder).
Before the BR23C in a 180-degree field of vision, the instrument identifies obstacles within a distance of up to 2 meters, calculates their distance and sends a signal to the on-board micro-processor, which is immediately transformed into an operation aimed at avoiding a collision.
“As soon as the obstacle is detected, the little robot mimiggers the uterus’s movements immediately change direction by moving the wheels at a smaller or larger angle in order to avoid a collision.
What distinguishes the system from the devices currently available is that in this case the bypass maneuver takes place completely automatically.
“If it wasn’t, the robot wouldn’t be able to react quickly enough to avoid obstacles,” explains Toshiyuki Andoh, director of the mobility Laboratory and chief engineer for the project.
Unlike bees, the manoeuvre can’t move up or down or diagonally, it can only move in two dimensional ways and only towards the swirability of the wheels, i.e. not in an infinite direction and manner, as happens in the case of bees.
That’s why, in addition to accelerating and decelerating, Nissan also used a rotational function to avoid collisions.
“This device only has to process and respond to the data every few seconds.
You don’t need to store your data, you don’t need an intuitive logic based on previous manoeuvres, so it can operate without interruption with just a few dozen KB of memory.
This may sound surprising, but it’s not really gigabytes, but we don’t even talk about megabyte-sized memories.
“It’s the first step to developing an Immediate Collision Prevention technology that Nissan estimates to enter production cars within ten years.”
The robot is powered by lithium-polymer batteries, which are lighter and more durable than the lithium-ion version.
In the near future, the integration of the unit with the GPS satellite navigation system could mean an additional possibility of appreciating the driver to the final destination by avoiding the obstacles on the road.
