Morgan Stanley Opens Capital for MUFG
Morgan Stanley has pledged its capital to the Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group (MUFG). The largest Japanese bank will pay USD 9 billion for 21% of the company’s capital, after Morgan Stanley’s share prices plummeted on the stock market.
A week after the publication of the preliminary agreement, the two banks said that MUFG would buy 9.9% of Morgan Stanley’s ordinary shares at a unit price of 25.25 dollars for a total of USD 3 billion.
Approximately 19% of the agreed price is less than the book value of securities on 31 August.
The MUFG is investing an additional USD 6 billion in the purchase of redeemable securities, which will provide it with a return of up to 10%, with an exchange rate of USD 31.25.
According to Morgan Stanley, when the global transaction is carried out, an average exchange rate of .228 percent higher than the current exchange rate.
However, Morgan Stanley’s share price fell by 6.79% at around 5 p.m. in Greenwich, closing at USD 23.7.7.
“This strategic federation is a great opportunity for Morgan Stanley to speed up its transformation into a bank-holding company,” explains John Mack, CEO of the U.S. group.
Earlier this week, Morgan Stanley endorsed a new status to diversify its activities, while at the same time falling under the control of the Federal Reserve.
The agreement with MUFG reaffirms that the current financial crisis requires even more cautiousness from corporate giants listed on Wall Street.
According to the conditions announced on Monday, the agreement has been amended in the last few days: last Monday Morgan only announced that it would sell 20% of its shares to the Japanese company for about 8.5 billion dollars.
“A week ago, we thought that the stakes would consist entirely of ordinary shares,” said Toshihide Mizuno, MUFG’s managing director in Tokyo.
“However, due to the current market and economic situation, we wanted to minimise and prevent potential risks.”
In terms of the weighted average price, MUFG pays 7% more than the book value for Morgan Stanley shares.
As a result of the transaction, the yield per share decreases by about 20%.
According to the agreement, MUFG can convert half of its preferred shares into ordinary shares within a year, provided that the trading price of the latter exceeds the exchange rate by more than 50% over a specified period, i.e. it rises to about USD 47.
Morgan Stanley thus ensures that you can use MUFG funds to take advantage of your investigative opportunities, improve your balance sheets and increase your liquid resources.
The group’s spokesperson, however, declined to provide accurate information about the strategic plans of the two new allies.
“Following MUFG’s alignment, Morgan Stanley is expected to increase its Tier I solvency rate by about 15%, making it one of the best in its sector.
Morgan has stated that its balance sheet has “definitely” improved since the end of August.
In the past few weeks, there has been a great ordequacy for the group, as the price of its shares has plummeted by half, until the cost of insurance against non-payment risk has skyrocketed.
This cost declined on Monday, but it remained high at around 12%.
Of the big investigative banks, there’s only one “survival” after the bankruptcy of Bear Stearns and Lehman Brothers and the acquisition of Merrill Lynch. This is Goldman Sachs, announcing last week’s purchase of USD 15 billion, some of which came from Berkshire Hathaway Warren Buffett.
Announcement by Rafael Correa, Quito
Recreation of the republic in the spirit of 21st-century socialism.
This is the joint plan for Hugo Chavez of Venezuela, Evo Morales of Bolivia and Rafael Correa in Ecuador.
Following a sample of his fellow-reversaries and friends, Correa expressed his ideas about the new constitution on Sunday.
He succeeded in fulfilling his mission as 64% of the electorate voted in favour in the referendum, according to the official results, which accounted for 80% of the votes.
According to this, 28% of the population voted against, 7% of the votes were invalid, while 0.7% were handed out unfilled.
Fifty + 1 vote was needed to adopt the draft constitution.
On Sunday, two polls at the exit of the ballot-government and a census carried out by an NGO both proved the huge superiority of “yyes” votes with 63% and 70%, respectively.
By the way, Rafael Correa already spoke about the victory of his draft constitution from Sunday to Monday.
“The constitution has achieved an overwhelming victory,” he said, thanking the entourants in Guayaquil, 280 kilometers southwest of Quito.
As it is well-founded, Rafael Correa was congratulated by Chavez, Morales and Chile’s President Michelle Bachelet.
According to a report from the Organization of the Organization of the United States (OAS), the vote took place in a relaxed atmosphere. Its main topic was a four4-point text summarizing the principles of "new socialism" defended by the president.
The proposed constitution puts state power at the service of the economy, withholding the state the right to “preparation of development” for a country that fundamentally benefits from oil extraction by foreign corporations and foreign currency sent by emigrants.
Correa’s draft constitution, who was elected in 2006, also reinforces the power of the head of state, who will be able to obtain a mandate for no more than two successive periods and is entitled to dissolving the National Assembly.
Correa has already announced that he intends to take advantage of this opportunity and will again compete in February 2009 for his mandate as head of state.
Health services and philosophial education have also been included in the constitution. The president has already begun its introduction in the country with a population of 1 3.9 million and half of the population living below the subsistence level.
Finally, the Constitution bans the presence of foreign troops in the country. This could put an end to Quito and Washington's agreement on the use of the U.S. anti-drug counterintelligence facility.
The 19 hostages were rescued in Chad
He was raided by an Egyptian commando on the robbers, who had taken eleven tourists and eight attendees hostage ten days ago.
The hostages are now in Cairo.
Eleven European tourists and their eight Egyptian escorts have been imprisoned in the desert for ten days, from where they were transported unscathed to the Egyptian capital on Monday afternoon.
The hostages were rescued by an Egyptian commando in Chad this morning.
Apparently in good condition, around 3:30 p.m. they arrived by plane to the al-Maza military base, which is directly connected to Cairo’s international airport.
The hostages were banned from speaking to the press and were immediately transported to a hospital in Maadi, a suburban residential area of Cairo, where they were examined.
“The hostages are “healthy” and “did not pay a ransom,” Zeheir Garanna, Egypt’s tourism minister, said at the airport.
The hostages were captured on 19 September at the foot of Mount Ouanat on the border between Egypt, Sudan and Libya, where they took part in an off-road safari. The group was then deported by 35 unfamiliar hostage-takers.
There were 11 tourists in the group (five Italian men, five German men and a Romanian woman), as well as eight Egyptian guides, four chauffeurs, a border keeper and Aegyptous travel agency.
According to an Egyptian security manager, a 30-member special commando arrived at the scene in two helicopters, where they raided the hostage-takers just before dawn.
“There was a firefight between the troops. The kidnappers were killed and the others escaped,” said the leader, who asked for his name to be withheld.
He also stated that a total of 150 special squadrons were sent from the Egyptian Villám brigade to Sudan, where they were supplemented with German and Italian commandos.
Franco Frattini, Italy’s long-suffituated the role of secret agents and special Italian action groups.
“We have to acknowledge that we would not have been able to achieve this result without the professional and effective intervention of our secret service personnel and action groups,” he said.
In addition, he denies that the hostages had been paid a ransom.
On the previous day, the Sudanese army announced an off-road vehicle on which the robberies traveled while patrolling. Six of them (including the Chadian leader) were arrested and two were arrested.
According to the army, the hostage-takers were in contact with a rebellious organization in Darfur, the Liberation Movement (SLA-U), which was immediately refuted.
