Or that there are no laws that really ensure the effective protection of our cultural heritage?
Did you know that the French government only spent three and a half a million euros on the refurbishment of Chinon Castle in the Loire Valley?
“The Constitution of our American Neighbours states that the protection of the cultural heritage is a part of American culture, and this heritage must be defended in a coordinated manner by all citizens, every elected representatives and all government agencies.
As part of the Association of Historical Societies (Fédération des sociétés d'histoire du Québec), more than 40,000 volunteers of 211 history, cultural heritage, archival and genealogy are engaged in the research of history, as well as the preservation of a large number of private archies and archaeological artifacts with a minimum budget.
Often these organizations are defending our tooth and vocal heritage, and with the last power of the drowning, they protest when real estate developers and decision-makers point their bulldozers towards our old buildings and forgotten cemeteries.
When are you going to stand up for the preservation of historical and cultural heritage?
The Benelux-governments nationalize part of the Fortis bank
Due to the spill-over effects of the U.S. credit market crisis, the Benelux-States are assisting Fortison, the Belgian-Netherlands Bank in Trouble, as the first in Europe: the three countries will support a total of 11.2 billion euros to the money and insurance institution, which also owns interests in Hungary, with a total of 11.2 billion euros.
The Belgian, Luxembourg and Dutch governments consulted on the matter at the highest level at the end of the week, involving the President of the European Central Bank and the European Commission’s competition officer.
In the euro area, Fortis has the first major crisis in the context of an international money market crisis that has been raging in waves for more than a year, so decisions in the case of the bank can be classified as a precedent-setting in some respects.
According to the Sunday night’s decision, nearly half (49%) of all three of the three states buy up the bank’s existing property.
The bank certainly sells some of its interests, including, above all, the activities that were purchased last year from ABN Amro (Netherlands).
Fortis’s shares started to fall sharply last week, and in the second half of the week there have already been serious crises at the institute.
Analysts agree, however, that with an employee of 85,000 people, the bank is too large to allow it to go bankrupt.
The company’s papers fell by about 12 percent to a 14-year low only on Friday.
Since January, the value of shares has declined by more than two-thirds.
With regard to the current share prices, the group’s total market capital value is around EUR 14 billion, but according to the company’s managers, this is unrealistic.
By the way, the company’s management blamed overseas market uncertainties related to the adoption of the 700-billion-dollar U.S. package plan for the fall of Fortis shares.
On Monday, the papers rose slightly.
On Friday Filip Dierckx, a new CEO was appointed head of the bank.
The financial institution was also looking for private buyers, but so far no one has applied with a substantive offer.
The Belgian government has already indicated that the investors do not have to worry, they do not lose their money.
So far, there has been no panic among the bank’s clients, the branches have not been assaulted.
“Fortis has a standalone account in Hungary and has also acquired leasing companies.
Frog in the henhouse
In the U., life and health insurers, based on people’s drug purchase data, often refuse to enter into an insurance contract.
Data protection experts fear that the data market will become uncontrollable.
“There is a data protection program that always link the use of prescridatory data to the consent of the person concerned.
“It has to be in an independent hand, or we’ll leave the heno to the fox,” said Richard Dick, the developer of a drug-prescribing program.
Fiscineration companies make the insurance premium and the amount due in the case of damage incidents dependent on the state of health of the applicant, and if they consider the risk to be too high, they do not even take out insurance with it.
In the past, the person was sent for a medical examination, and the contract was concluded only in the light of the results of the examination.
Ten years ago, Dick came up with a much cheaper procedure: based according to the customer’s identification number, pharmacies are easily procured by what kind of drugs the person has purchased in the last year and a half, and the program itself deduces what kind of illness he suffers from and what his medical condition is.
For example, if you regularly take a large dose of cholesterol-lowering, it suggests that you have virtually unmanageably high levels of fat in the blood, which immediately categorizes it into a higher risk category.
There are several versions of the program in circulation, some of them also set risk multipliers, so the insurer has hardly anything left to do in addition to paperwork, although there are minor difficulties raised.
Disadvantages include that some of the drugs can be used for several purposes, e.g. they can be used against migraines or heatwaves, but they are also effective against depression.
The latter constitutes a risk-enhancing factor for business insurers, the former are not.
Separate companies have been established to retrieve and process the data, and before they obtain the necessary information from pharmacies, they ask for the patient’s consent.
The authorities are now obliging them to do so, even though they have not been punished for their occupations so far.
Recently, some of the companies have already processed the results of laboratory examinations and are also associated with drug consumption data, so they are able to provide an even more accurate picture of the patient’s state of health.
More and more companies have access to the data, and at the same time it becomes more and more diversified.
There’s a separate health data protection law, but it only applies to healthcare providers and insurers.
Prior to the congress, there is a bill that also gives the authorities the power to control over companies that specialise in the provision of data.
Dick Richard observes that this is not enough: he has therefore further developed his programme, which would link the transmission of the data to the explicit consent of the data subject.
It is another matter that the person concerned can hardly deny the consent, otherwise the insurers will not enter into a contract with him.
So the hens are somewhat relied on the fox’s benevolence.
